# The projection manifest fixtures are DIGEST-BEARING CONTENT, not text.
#
# Generation 2 names generation 1 by a sha256 over generation 1's exact bytes, and generation 3 names
# generation 2 the same way. That chain is the thing the succession rules are built on, so a checkout that
# rewrote LF to CRLF would change the bytes, change the digest, and break the chain — on Windows only, with a
# failure that reads like a contract defect rather than like a line-ending conversion.
#
# This is deliberately the NARROWEST possible rule. It covers one fixture directory whose files are content
# addressed by their own bytes. It is not a repository-wide line-ending policy: every other file in this
# repository is still handled exactly as it was, and the CRLF-tolerance the rest of the suites were written
# for is unchanged. `test/projection-manifest-v1.ts` asserts these files really did arrive without CR, so a
# future change that defeats this rule fails by name instead of by digest.
test/fixtures/projection-manifest-v1/** -text

# Go sources are LF, on every platform.
#
# gofmt treats CRLF as a formatting difference, so on a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true every Go file
# would arrive with CRLF and `gofmt -l` — run inside the Linux gate container against the bind-mounted working
# copy — would list all of them. That is a gate failing for a reason that has nothing to do with the code.
*.go text eol=lf
projectiond/go.mod text eol=lf
projectiond/go.sum text eol=lf
projectiond/internal/contract/contract.generated.json text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are LF, on every platform, because a CRLF one is not a script.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS FIXES, MEASURED RATHER THAN ANTICIPATED. On a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true
# these arrive with CRLF. Git Bash tolerates that; WSL's bash does not, and `bash` on a Windows PATH is
# routinely WSL. Running the three-run wrapper there produces:
#
#     set: pipefail\r: invalid option name
#     syntax error near unexpected token `$'do\r''
#
# ...and an exit status of 2. What that looked like from the offline suite was four WRAPPER-ACCOUNTING tests
# failing — the tests whose whole subject is that a SKIPPED RUN MUST NOT LOOK LIKE A PASSING ONE. So the most
# important property in this tranche appeared broken, from PowerShell, because of a line ending; and it
# appeared fine from Git Bash, which is the worst version of the same problem: a verdict that depends on
# which shell you happened to start.
#
# It is not only a test concern. These scripts are meant to run on the Linux or Unraid host where the tranche
# actually closes, and a CRLF copy carried there fails the same way.
*.sh text eol=lf
